  "query_preview": "day by day as heat remains with little change in the\npattern. Another thing that will come from these hot and dry\nconditions is the return of critical fire weather conditions.\nWhile most areas look to remain localized, the Four Corners\nvalleys will see some increased gusty winds on Monday afternoon\nas a shortwave trough passes to the north and allows for some\ndeeper mixing. Wind gusts are projected to be in the 25 to 35\nmph range for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado valleys with\nRH in the single digits to low teens, so went ahead and issued a\nRed Flag Warning for Monday for UTZ491 and COZ207 from noon to 8\npm MDT. A few more passing shortwaves look to move across the\nNorthern Rockies dropping over the Front Range. While we remain\nhot and dry, there is a non-zero chance of isolated virga\nshowers and/or thunderstorms mainly along the eastern San Juans\nand up the Divide, but most activity should remain east. Wetting\nrain chances appear non-existent in this hot and dry air mass.\n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nLooking further out, toward the end of the workweek and the coming \nweekend, ensemble and deterministic guidance seem inclined to bring \na trough into the West Coast. This would finally displace the high \nto the east, potentially allowing moisture to be drawn up into the \nFour Corners once more. Models were indicating this moisture and\nstorm increase potentially happening late this week but have \nreally backed off and pushed this back to late this coming \nweekend into next week. This will be something to watch over \nthe coming days as forecast confidence increases but confidence\nis low at this time.",
